---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const CONTACT_EMAIL = "hello@digitaldramaa.com";
---

<Layout
  title="Editorial Policy — DigitalDramaa"
  description="How DigitalDramaa's Commentary section is written, reviewed, and corrected — and how AI is and isn't used."
  keywords={["DigitalDramaa", "editorial policy", "corrections policy"]}
  monetize
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Editorial</span>
    <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
    <p>How Commentary gets made, and how to flag a problem.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Archive vs. Commentary</h2>
    <p>
      DigitalDramaa has two sections. The <a href="/blog">Archive</a> is an
      automated headline tracker — no rewritten article body, no AI-generated
      opinion, and it isn't indexed by search engines. <a href="/commentary">Commentary</a>
      is where original analysis is actually published, under the
      DigitalDramaa Editorial byline. This policy is about Commentary.
    </p>

    <h2>How AI Is Used</h2>
    <p>
      AI can help find stories, gather sources, and draft structure. It does
      not publish anything on its own, and it does not write opinions about
      real people without a human editor reviewing them first. AI-written
      content isn't the problem being managed here — content that adds
      nothing beyond a source article is, so every Commentary post is
      expected to add context, history, or analysis that isn't already in
      the source reporting.
    </p>

    <h2>Claims About Real People</h2>
    <p>
      Disputed or unconfirmed claims about named individuals are written as
      alleged and attributed to their source ("according to X"), never
      stated as settled fact. Commentary criticizes actions, not people's
      character, and doesn't call for shaming or pile-ons. Every post naming
      an individual is reviewed and approved by a human editor before it
      publishes. We generally avoid covering private individuals who went
      viral by circumstance, focusing instead on public figures, companies,
      and platforms.
    </p>

    <h2>Sourcing</h2>
    <p>
      Every Commentary post cites its sources explicitly and links to them.
      Where a story draws on multiple outlets, we combine them into one
      piece rather than publishing near-duplicate posts per source, and
      quote sparingly rather than reproducing large chunks of someone else's
      reporting.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If something on the site is inaccurate, misattributed, or you're the
      subject of a post and want to flag a concern, email{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the link
      and a short description of the issue. We review correction requests
      directly and update or remove content when warranted — see our{" "}
      <a href="/contact">Contact page</a> for other ways to reach us.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding: var(--sp-32) 0 var(--sp-24);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    margin-bottom: var(--sp-32);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 3px 9px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
  }

  .page-head p {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 72ch;
    padding-bottom: var(--sp-64);
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    margin: var(--sp-32) 0 0.6rem;
  }

  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .prose p {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .prose a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
